AB  - This study proposes the bounty hunter optimizer (BHO), a novel optimization algorithm inspired by the behavior of bounty hunters. Unlike traditional methods that rely on mean aggregation, BHO employs a decentralized position-update strategy based on local differences and random disturbances, inherently avoiding center bias and population collapse. BHO introduces three core innovations: (1) the Explorpolis rule, which quantifies individual fitness and its spatial relationship to the high-quality solution set, enabling intelligent filtering of inferior solutions; (2) a quantum probability rotation selection mechanism, which replaces the traditional roulette-wheel selection using quantum bits and rotation gates to continuously adjust probability amplitudes, significantly enhancing population diversity; and (3) an evolutionary model inspired by the "thorough investigation," "rough search," and "hunter re-assignment" behaviors of hunters, integrated with a self-feedback adjustment mechanism to dynamically balance exploration and exploitation. Experimental results on the CEC2017 test suite demonstrate that the BHO excels in both 30-dimensional and 100-dimensional optimization tasks, significantly outperforming 11 advanced optimization algorithms, including four competition-level state-of-the-art variants. When applied to the multi-unmanned aerial vehicle (multi-UAV) mobile edge computing (MEC) and path planning (PP) (multi-UAV MEC-PP) system, BHO effectively addresses the path optimization problem under quality-of-service constraints. Extensive simulations across different scales confirm its superior performance, achieving >15% cost reduction relative to advanced competitors and providing reliable and efficient support for PP and decision-making in MEC environments.
