AB  - The convergence of aerial computing and edge intelligence has significantly increased the demand for task offloading, placing considerable strain on limited wireless and edge resources. This paper proposes a lightweight collaborative scheduling algorithm for heterogeneous edge systems, where Industrial Internet of Things devices (IoTDs) partially offload computational tasks to multiple unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) equipped with edge capabilities. The problem is formulated as a multi-stage stochastic Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Programming (MINLP) problem that jointly optimizes task partitioning, transmission power, local computing frequency, and multi-UAV trajectories under dynamic channel, mobility, and energy constraints. Using Lyapunov optimization, the long-term problem is decomposed into per-slot deterministic subproblems, ensuring queue stability and bounded average energy consumption. Each subproblem is modeled as a Multi-Agent Markov Decision Process (MDP), in which the reward function incorporates both the overall system energy consumption cost and the difference rewards among multiple agents. We develop a Multi-Agent Deep Reinforcement Learning (MADRL) algorithm based on the Actor-Critic(AC) framework, integrating target networks and dual estimators to enhance training stability. A stepwise epsilon-greedy strategy is employed to balance exploration and exploitation. The proposed framework supports centralized training with decentralized execution, enabling scalable multi-UAV coordination. The simulation results demonstrate an improved convergence speed, reduced energy consumption, and more efficient UAV trajectory planning compared to baseline methods.
